          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            The
            %em Higgs field
            or
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Higgs+boson"} Higgs boson
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/scalar+field"} scalar
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/physical+field"} physical field
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fundamental+particle"} fundamental particle
            in a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+theory"} gauge theory
            such as the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/standard+model+of+particle+physics"} standard model of particle physics
            supposedly responsible for the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spontaneously+broken+symmetry"} spontaneously broken symmetry
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/electroweak+field"} electroweak field
            (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/electroweak+symmetry+breaking"}> electroweak symmetry breaking
            ) and for giving elementary particles their
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mass"} masses
            by the
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Higgs+mechanism"} Higgs mechanism
          %h2#properties Properties
          %h3#MassAndVacuumInstability Mass and vacuum (in-)stability
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/rest+mass"} rest mass
            of the Higgs particle observed at the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/LHC"} LHC
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/experiment"} experiment
            is about
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 125
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 125
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/GeV"} GeV
            (
            %a{:href => "#ATLAS12"}> ATLAS Collaboration 12
            ,
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#CMS12"} CMS Collaboration 12
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#ATLAS15"} ATLAS Collaboration 15
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#Gibbs11a"} Gibbs 11a
            %a{:href => "#HLL11"} HLL 11
            for early discussion).
          %p
            This is determined by a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/local+minimum"} local minimum
            of the Higgs
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/potential+energy"} potential
            (see
            %a{:href => "#Kusenko15"} Kusenko 15
            for exposition):
          %p
            %img{:src => "/nlab/files/HiggsPotentials.png", :width => "500px"}/
          %p#Curiously
            Curiously, the Higgs
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/potential+energy"} potential
            is such that the Higgs field at this mass is at least close to being at the border between
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vacuum+stability"} vacuum stability
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/false+vacuum"} false vacuum
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#EEGHR09"} EEGHR 09
            = succeed "):" do
              %a{:href => "#Gibbs11b"} Gibbs 11b
          %p
            %img{:src => "/nlab/files/HiggsVacuumStabilityIII.png", :width => "700px"}/
          %p
            Then it was amplified again after the detection of the Higgs particle at the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/LHC"} LHC
            (
            %a{:href => "#DVEEGI12"}> DVEEGI 12
            ):
          %p
            %img{:src => "/nlab/files/HiggsVacuumStability.png", :width => "700px"}/
          %p
            More detailed computation at
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-loop"} 2-loop
            confirmed this result, showing that the observed Higgs vacuum is indeed very close to the boundary between the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vacuum+stability"} stable
            and the meta-stable region (
            %a{:href => "#BKPV15"}> BKPV 15
            ):
          %p
            %img{:src => "/nlab/files/HiggsVacuumStabilityII.png", :width => "400px"}/
          %p#SeeAlsoAFS18
            See also
            = succeed ":" do
              %a{:href => "#AFS18"} AFS 18
          %p
            %img{:src => "/nlab/files/HiggsVacuumStability4.png", :width => "800px"}/
          %p
            In summary (
            %a{:href => "#Kusenko15"}> Kusenko 15
            ):
          %blockquote
            %p
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [
              The
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} ]
              conclusion is that the best theoretical fit to measured parameters, including the Higgs and top-quark masses, points to a metastable Universe. However, their analysis also concludes that values of parameters are closer to a region of absolute stability than suggested by previous studies: it is possible for the Universe to be fully stable (and for the standard model to work all the way up to the Planck scale), if the true values of measured parameters are only 1.3 standard deviations away from the current best estimates.
          %p
            And more recently (
            %a{:href => "#PDG18"}> PDG 18, p. 9
            ):
          %blockquote
            %p#PDG18OnVacuumMetastability
              However, for the value of Higgs mass experimentally measured, the EW vacuum of the Higgs potential is most likely metastable. Indeed, the high energy evolution of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi λ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \lambda
              shows that it becomes
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/negative+number"} negative
              at
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/energies"} energies
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Λ
                    %mo =
                    %mi 𝒪
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msup
                      %mn 10
                      %mn 10
                    %mo −
                    %msup
                      %mn 10
                      %mn 12
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Lambda = \mathcal{O}(10^{10}-10^{12})
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/GeV"} GeV
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/top+quark"} top quark
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mass"} mass
              exceeds its current measured value by
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/statistical+significance"} 3σ
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/standard+model+of+particle+physics"} SM
              Higgs potential develops an instability and the long term existence of the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/electroweak+symmetry+breaking"} EW
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vacuum"} vacuum
              is challenged. This behavior may call for new physics at an intermediate scale before the instability develops, i.e., below
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Planck+mass"} M_Planck
          %p
            However
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+tunneling"} quantum tunneling
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vacuum+decay"} vacuum decay
            is an intrinsically
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/non-perturbative+effect"} non-perturbative effect
            which needs careful treatment beyond
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbative+quantum+field+theory"} perturbative quantum field theory
            (e.g.
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "#AFFS17"} AFFS 17
          %div{:style => "text-align: center"}
            %img{:src => "/nlab/files/NonPerturbativeTunneling.jpg", :width => "600px"}/
          %blockquote
            %p
              graphics grabbed form
              %a{:href => "#Schwartz15"} Schwartz 15, slide 44
          %p
            %br/
          %h3#PossibleCausesOfNEarCriticality Possible causes of the near criticality
          %p
            Given, by the
            %a{:href => "#MassAndVacuumInstability"} above
            discussion, that the parameters of the Higgs field are observed to be inside a special reason of their potential parameter space, it is natural to speculate that there is some mechanism that enforces or at least prefers this position.
          %p
            In
            %a{:href => "#BDGGSSS13"} BDGGSSS 13, Section 5.2
            is speculation that for dynamics over a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/landscape+of+string+theory+vacua"} landscape of vacua
            critical points may generally serve as attractors.
          %p
            More specifically,
            %a{:href => "#IsidoriPattori17"} Isidori-Pattori 17
            claims that, under reasonable assumptions (including
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+coupling+unification"} gauge coupling unification
            = succeed "\u201D)" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/naturalness"} naturalness
            = succeed "-extensions" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supersymmetry"} supersymmetric
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/standard+model+of+particle+physics"} standard model of particle physics
            (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/MSSM"}> MSSM
            ) predict a parameter range for the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/top+quark"} top quark
            and Higgs field
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mass"} mass
            that is close to coinciding with the corresponding parameter space for Higgs field near criticality:
          %div{:style => "text-align: center"}
            %img{:src => "/nlab/files/IsidoriPattoriViable.jpg", :width => "600px"}/
          %blockquote
            %p
              graphics grabbed from
              %a{:href => "#IsidoriPattori17"} Isidori-Pattori 17
          %p
            That
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supersymmetry"} supersymmetry
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/G%E2%82%82-MSSM"} G₂-MSSM
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#Kane18"} Kane 18, “Clue 4”
          %h3#CosmologicalInstability Cosmological instability?
          %p
            However, it has been argued that an actual
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/false+vacuum"} false vacuum
            of the Higgs is incompatible with
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cosmology"} cosmology
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vacuum+fluctuations"} vacuum fluctuations
            during
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/inflation"} inflation
            the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vacuum+decay"} vacuum decay
            would not have been avoided (
            %a{:href => "#EGR07"}> EGR 07
            ,
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#HKSZ14"} HKSZ 14
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#EGMRSST15"} EGMRSST 15
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#EKSYZ16"} EKSYZ 16
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#BDGGSSS13"} BDGGSSS 13, section 7
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "#Kane18"} Kane 18 , “Clue 4”
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supersymmetry"} supersymmetry
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#Gibbs11b"} Gibbs 11b
          %h3#AsymtoticSafetyOrNot Asymptotic safety?
          %p
            The near criticality of the Higgs field
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vacuum"} vacuum
            discussed
            %a{:href => "#MassAndVacuumInstability"} above
            implies that the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coefficient"} coefficient
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi λ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \lambda
            of the quartic part of the Higgs potential is close to zero after
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/renormalization+group+flow"} renormalization group flow
            (“RGE”) to around the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Planck+scale"} Planck scale
            of about
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mn 10
                    %mn 19
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 10^{19}
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/GeV"} GeV
            (e.g.
            = succeed "):" do
              %a{:href => "#BDGGSSS13"} BDGGSSS 13, p. 17-18
          %p
            %img{:src => "/nlab/files/HiggsQuarticCoupling.png", :width => "400px"}/
          %p
            In fact also the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/beta+function"} beta function
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi β
                    %mi λ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \beta_\lambda
            of the quartic coupling
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi λ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \lambda
            (i.e. its logarithmic
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/derivative"} derivative
            with respect to
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/scale"} scale
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Planck+scale"} Planck scale
            of about
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mn 10
                    %mn 19
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 10^{19}
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/GeV"} GeV
            (
            %a{:href => "#BDGGSSS13"}> BDGGSSS 13, p. 18
            ):
          %p
            %img{:src => "/nlab/files/HiggsQuarticBetaFunctionRelative.png", :width => "400px"}/
          %p
            Earlier it has been suggested that this reflects the principle of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/asymptotic+safety"} asymptotic safety
            (
            %a{:href => "#ShaposhnikovWetterich09"}> Shaposhnikov-Wetterich 09
            ). But this would mean that not only
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi λ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \lambda
            and its
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/beta+function"} RGE-derivative
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi β
                    %mi λ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \beta_\lambda
            vanish around the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Planck+scale"} Planck scale
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "asymptotic+safety#Niedermaier06"} Niedermaier 06, equation (1.5)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi β
                    %mi λ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \beta_\lambda
            asymptotes to zero. But this does not seem to be the case; in (
            %a{:href => "#BDGGSSS13"}> BDGGSSS 13, p. 17-18
            ) it says:
          %blockquote
            %p
              As shown in fig. 2 (upper right), the corresponding Higgs quartic
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/beta-function"} beta-function
              vanishes at a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/scale"} scale
              of about
              = succeed "-" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mn 10
                        %mn 17
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 10^{17}
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mn 10
                      %mn 18
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 10^{18}
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/GeV"} GeV
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi β
                      %mi λ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \beta_\lambda
              in units of its pure
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/top+quark"} top quark
              contribution. The vanishing of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi β
                      %mi λ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \beta_\lambda
              looks more like an accidental cancellation between various large contributions, rather than an asymptotic approach to zero.
          %p
            %img{:src => "/nlab/files/HiggsQuarticBetaFunction.png", :width => "800px"}/
          %h3#HiggsInflation Higgs inflation?
          %p
            Starting with
            %a{:href => "cosmic+inflation#Jegerlehner13"} Jegerlehner 13
            has been argued that the near-criticality of the Higgs potential is in fact consistent or even necessary for
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Higgs+inflation"} Higgs inflation
            to be a viable model. See
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "cosmic+inflation#Jegerlehner18"} Jegerlehner 18
          %h2#models Models
          %p There is no lack of proposals for realizing the Higgs field in various big schemes of mathematical structures modelling physics.
          %h3#gaugehiggs_unification Gauge-Higgs unification
          %p
            A mechanism, of
            %em gauge-Higgs unification
            (
            %a{:href => "#Manton79"}> Manton 79
            ,
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "#Hosotani83"} Hosotani 83
            %em Hosotani mechanism
            (see
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "#Hosotani12"} Hosotani 12
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+%28in+theoretical+physics%29"} models
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/particle+physics"} particle physics
            where the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Higgs+field"} Higgs field
            on 4d
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spacetimes"} spacetimes
            arises as a component of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+field"} gauge field
            in higher dimensions, either after
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kaluza-Klein+compactification"} Kaluza-Klein compactification
            or by passage to a 4d
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/asymptotic+boundary"} asymptotic boundary
            in a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Randall-Sundrum+model"} Randall-Sundrum model
            or similar.
          %center
            %img{:src => "https://ncatlab.org/nlab/files/GaugeHiggsUnification.jpg", :width => "500"}/
          %blockquote
            %p
              graphics grabbed from
              %a{:href => "#Lim09"} Lim 09
          %p
            Gauge-Higgs unification is naturally combined with
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/GUT"} grand unification
            of gauge groups, whence one then also speaks of
            %em gauge-Higgs grand unification
            (e.g.
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "#HosotaniYamatsu15"} Hosotani-Yamatsu 15
          %p
            Also the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Connes-Lott+models"} Connes-Lott models
            realize gauge-Higgs unification, in a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/non-commutative+geometry"} non-commutative geometric
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/KK-compactification"} KK-compactification
          %h3#in_string_theory In string theory
          %p
            In
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+theory"} string theory
            (see
            = succeed ")" do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+phenomenology"} string phenomenology
          %h4#in_intersecting_dbrane_models In intersecting D-brane models
          %p
            Notably in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/intersecting+D-brane+models"} intersecting D-brane models
            it arises naturally from brane recombination at intersections: (
            %a{:href => "#CremadesIbanezMarchesano02"}> Cremades-Ibanez-Marchesano 02, section 7
            ):
          %p
            %img{:src => "/nlab/files/BraneRecombination.png", :width => "600px"}/
          %p
            Further developments in
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#IbanezMarchesanoRabadan01"} Ibanez-Marchesano-Rabadan 01
            %a{:href => "#HebeckerKnochelWeigand13"} Hebecker-Knochel-Weigand 13
            and specifically via
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+field+theory"} string field theory
            in
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#HINSS18"} HINSS 18
          %p
            For review see
            = succeed ":" do
              %a{:href => "intersecting+D-brane+model#IbanezUranga12"} Ibanez-Uranga 12, fig 10.2
          %h4#in_mssm
            In
            = succeed "-MSSM" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi G
                      %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G_2
          %p
            Discussion of the Higgs mechanism in the
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/G%E2%82%82-MSSM"} G₂-MSSM
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#Kane11"} Kane 11
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#KaneKumarLuZheng11"} Kane-Kumar-Lu-Zheng 11
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#Kane17"} Kane 17
            %a{:href => "#Kane18"} Kane 18
          %h3#other Other
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                in the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/technicolor"} technicolor
                model the Higgs field is not a fundamental particle but a compound of
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fermions"} fermions
                = succeed ";" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+theory"} gauge theory
            %li
              %p
                in
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Connes-Lott-Chamseddine-Barrett+models"} Connes-Lott-Chamseddine-Barrett models
                it has been
                %a{:href => "standard+model+of+particle+physics#NCGeometry"} shown
                that the Higgs may be modeled as a component of the gauge bosons assuming that the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kaluza-Klein+mechanism"} KK-reduction
                is over a certain non-commutative space of classical dimension 0.
          %h2#history History
          %p
            The Higgs mechanism was proposed in 1963-1964 by a fair number of authors essentially simultaneously, see the
            %a{:href => "#References"} References
            below. The explicit prediction of the Higgs boson implied by this mechanism though seems to be solely due to (
            %a{:href => "#Higgs64"}> Higgs 64
            ).
          %p
            The Higgs boson (or at least something very much like it) was finally detected in 2013 at the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/LHC"} LHC
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/experiment"} experiment
          %p
            So for the Higgs particle prediction and experimental detection lie apart by about 50 years. Compare maybe to the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/neutrino"} neutrino
          %h2#related_concepts Related concepts
          %ul
